{"causes": ["The decline of progressive rock and glam rock in the mid-1970s created a vacuum for a new sound.", "The DIY ethic of the punk rock movement inspired musicians to form bands and record independently.", "A growing youth culture in the UK sought music that reflected their rebellious spirit and desire for authenticity."], "effects": ["The NWOBHM led to the mainstream acceptance of heavy metal music in the 1980s, paving the way for bands to reach international audiences.", "It inspired a wave of metal subgenres, including thrash metal, speed metal, and power metal, which emerged in the following decades.", "The movement fostered a dedicated fan culture, resulting in the formation of music festivals and conventions celebrating heavy metal music. "], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "heavy-metal", "sourceName": "Heavy Metal", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "new-wave-of-british-heavy-metal", "targetName": "New Wave of British Heavy Metal", "context": "The evolution of heavy metal in the 1970s set the stage for the emergence of the NWOBHM."}, {"sourceSlug": "punk-rock", "sourceName": "Punk Rock", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "new-wave-of-british-heavy-metal", "targetName": "New Wave of British Heavy Metal", "context": "The DIY approach of punk rock inspired the bands that defined the NWOBHM."}, {"sourceSlug": "marquee-club-london", "sourceName": "Marquee Club, London", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "new-wave-of-british-heavy-metal", "targetName": "New Wave of British Heavy Metal", "context": "The Marquee Club served as a critical venue for NWOBHM bands in the late 1970s and early 1980s."}, {"sourceSlug": "iron-maiden", "sourceName": "Iron Maiden", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "new-wave-of-british-heavy-metal", "targetName": "New Wave of British Heavy Metal", "context": "Iron Maiden is one of the defining bands of the NWOBHM, shaping its sound and aesthetic."}, {"sourceSlug": "judas-priest", "sourceName": "Judas Priest", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "new-wave-of-british-heavy-metal", "targetName": "New Wave of British Heavy Metal", "context": "Judas Priest's innovations in guitar work and stage presence were instrumental in the NWOBHM."}], "places": [{"name": "London, United Kingdom", "role": "Cultural Hub"}, {"name": "Birmingham, United Kingdom", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Nottingham, United Kingdom", "role": "Major Venue"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: new wave of British heavy metal", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "new-wave-of-british-heavy-metal"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q370515", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_wave_of_British_heavy_metal"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "new wave of British heavy metal contributed to the historical record of the Contemporary period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The NWOBHM significantly shaped the heavy metal genre, influencing countless bands and solidifying a global fan base that continues to thrive today.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-02T05:57:52Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28568530456", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Heavy metal movement of the late 1970s and early 1980s Created c. 1978.", "newValue": "Emerging in the late 1970s, the new wave of British heavy metal (NWOBHM) represents a pivotal moment in the evolution of rock music, characterized by its distinct sound, aggressive guitar riffs, and energetic performances. This movement, which flourished primarily in the United Kingdom, saw bands like Iron Maiden, Judas Priest, and Motörhead redefine heavy metal and influence countless artists worldwide. The NWOBHM created a cultural phenomenon that not only reinvigorated the heavy metal genre but also laid the groundwork for various subgenres that followed, marking a significant chapter in the history of contemporary music.

The NWOBHM can be traced back to the late 1970s, around 1978, when a group of bands rejected the mainstream rock trends of the time, instead embracing a rawer, more aggressive sound. Key events included the release of landmark albums such as Iron Maiden's self-titled debut in 1980 and Judas Priest's "British Steel" in 1980, which showcased faster tempos and more complex guitar work than their predecessors. The influential fanzine, "Metal Forces," and the iconic venue, the Marquee Club in London, played crucial roles in promoting these bands and solidifying their place in the music scene. The movement's impact was felt internationally, as it inspired the heavy metal revival in the United States and influenced the development of thrash metal and other subgenres in the 1980s.

The legacy of the NWOBHM is profound, as it not only transformed heavy metal into a global phenomenon but also led to the emergence of a dedicated fan base that persists to this day. Many NWOBHM bands have continued to tour and release new music, influencing newer bands in the process. The movement's themes of rebellion and individuality resonate in contemporary music and culture, demonstrating its far-reaching consequences. The NWOBHM has become a cultural touchstone, with its artists often cited as foundational figures in heavy metal history."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-02T05:57:52Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28568530456", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Cultural, artistic, or intellectual creation"], "newValue": ["The decline of progressive rock and glam rock in the mid-1970s created a vacuum for a new sound.", "The DIY ethic of the punk rock movement inspired musicians to form bands and record independently.", "A growing youth culture in the UK sought music that reflected their rebellious spirit and desire for authenticity."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-02T05:57:52Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28568530456", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission"], "newValue": ["The NWOBHM led to the mainstream acceptance of heavy metal music in the 1980s, paving the way for bands to reach international audiences.", "It inspired a wave of metal subgenres, including thrash metal, speed metal, and power metal, which emerged in the following decades.", "The movement fostered a dedicated fan culture, resulting in the formation of music festivals and conventions celebrating heavy metal music. "]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-02T05:57:52Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28568530456", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "new-wave-of-british-heavy-metal", "sourceName": "new wave of British heavy metal", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "era-contemporary", "targe